Writer: a large plain text file is laid out page by page instead of moving every paragraph on each page break.

Text files have no layout cache, and upstream removed the paragraphs-per-page guess in SwLayHelper
(tdf#166871), so all paragraphs of a newly opened .txt are created on the first page. Each page break
then moves the whole rest of the document to the next page: SwFrame::GetNextLeaf() walks all following
frames, SwFlowFrame::PasteTree() reparents them (CheckDirChange for each) and SwLayAction visits them
again. That is O(paragraphs x pages): a 50 MB file of short lines took about 20 minutes of full layout,
93 % of the samples in those loops, and the window stopped answering for the whole time.

For documents opened by the plain text filters (TEXT, TEXT_DLG) with at least 3000 paragraphs, no
header, footer or columns, SwLayHelper now inserts a page when the paragraphs on the current page are
estimated to fill the body. Each paragraph is measured with its font on the reference device (line
height, character advances, lines of 90 % of the body width, line and paragraph spacing) and the result
is increased by a tenth, so the pages start slightly underfilled and the normal layout pulls the
following paragraphs back; every move is bounded by one page. (An estimate below half of the real page
would leave empty pages behind the layout, one above it would push a growing rest from page to page.)
The final pagination is the same as before; the new test compares every page start with the
start-to-end layout (HST_SW_TEXT_PAGINATION=0 turns the estimate off for that).

Pulling paragraphs back briefly empties pages in the middle, and removing such a page (or creating the
pre-estimated pages) recomputed the position of every page and moved all their frames each time
(SwRootFrame::CheckViewLayout), and changed the left/right format of every following page, each again
with CheckViewLayout. For such documents only (SwRootFrame::IsLargeTextPagination), CheckPageDescs,
RemoveSuperfluous and the frame creation now compute the page positions once at the end of their run
(SwViewLayoutDeferGuard), and SwLayAction's outer loop limit, one run per page, grows with the page
count instead of interrupting every layout pass of a text over 10000 pages. The pages that pulling
back leaves empty between the layout and the rest of the text are removed every 128 pages
(SwLayAction::InternalAction runs the pending CheckPageDescs) instead of at the end of the pass, so
later moves no longer walk across a growing run of empty pages. Other documents are unchanged: the
test also saves the large text as .odt and .docx and checks that reloading them stays on the old path.

